[97.840577, "o", "Fixed. Here's what was wrong and what I did.\r\n\r\n## Root cause\r\n\r\nYour default `python3` is a source build at `/usr/local/bin/python3.13` (CPython 3.13.1). Its site-packages directory had been gutted:\r\n\r\n- `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip/` — **the entire pip package was gone** (~840 files)\r\n- `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info/` — the metadata was still there\r\n- `/usr/local/bin/pip` and `pip3` still existed and still pointed at `python3.13`\r\n\r\nSo every `pip` invocation died at import time:\r\n\r\n```\r\nModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe site-packages directory's mtime was `Oct 31 13:45`, i.e. someone/something deleted the `pip/` directory (a partial uninstall or a stray `rm -rf`) without touching the dist-info or the console scripts.\r\n\r\n## Fix\r\n\r\n1. Moved the orphaned `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` to `/tmp` — without this, the bootstrap sees \"pip 24.3.1 already installed\" and silently does nothing.\r\n2. Ran `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip`, whi"]
[97.840813, "o", "ch reinstalled pip 24.3.1 from the interpreter's own bundled wheel (`ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl`) — no network needed.\r\n\r\n## Verification\r\n\r\n- `python3 -m pip --version` → `pip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)`\r\n- `/usr/local/bin/pip --version` → same (console scripts work again)\r\n- `import pip` succeeds; `pip/` now has 841 files\r\n- End-to-end test: downloaded `six`, installed it with `pip install`, and imported it successfully — index access and installation both work\r\n- Temp test dirs and the `/tmp` backup were cleaned up\r\n\r\n## Two things worth knowing\r\n\r\n- **There's a second interpreter with no pip:** `/usr/bin/python3` is Debian's Python 3.11.2. It has neither `pip` nor `ensurepip` (Debian strips both). That's normal for Debian, not damage — install `python3-pip` via `apt` if you ever need that interpreter, and don't run `get-pip.py` against it, which can conflict with apt-managed packages.\r\n- **pip itself is a bit old:** 24.3.1, and it no"]
[97.840852, "o", "tices a newer 26.2.2. I left it alone since upgrading wasn't part of the request — run `python3 -m pip install --upgrade pip` when you want it. Also note you're operating as root, so pip warns about system-wide installs; for project work a virtualenv (`python3 -m venv .venv`) is the safer default.\r\n\r\nSince `pip` was working before Oct 31, whatever removed that directory could recur (e.g. a cleanup script or tool that manages `/usr/local/lib`). If it happens again, `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` after clearing the stale dist-info is the whole repair.\r\n"]
